# Agent Rules — Binding on Any Agent Working in This Repo

This applies equally to a human contributor and any AI agent (this
session, a future one, a different provider) that opens a PR, pushes a
commit, or otherwise acts on **{{PROJECT_NAME}}**. It exists so the repo
stays safe and predictable regardless of *who* — or *what* — is doing the
work, and so a newly onboarded agent has a single place to read before
touching anything.

These are hard rules, not defaults to weigh against convenience. If a rule
below would block something that genuinely needs to happen, that's a
conversation to have with a human owner (`.github/CODEOWNERS`), not a
reason to route around the rule.

## Stage 0 — before any Phase 1 code exists

**If `STAGE-0-RESEARCH-ENRICHMENT.md` in this repo has no completed exit
sign-off yet, stop and run it before writing Phase 1 (first real,
load-bearing) code.** This applies to a brand-new project, or a genuinely
new product/feature direction within an existing one — not routine
feature work on an already-cleared product, which stays governed by the
rest of this document instead. See that file for the full process; the
two things worth knowing before you open it:

- **Legal/compliance research documented there never blocks code** — only
  the live execution of a specifically legally-gated action waits on a
  real answer, and only that action.
- **A missing external account/endpoint never blocks code either** — build
  against a pluggable interface, connect the real thing once it exists.

If `STAGE-0-RESEARCH-ENRICHMENT.md` doesn't exist in this repo yet and
this is a new product direction, create it from
`templates/STAGE-0-RESEARCH-ENRICHMENT.md.template` and run the process
before continuing — don't skip it because the pressure is to start
building.

## Git operations

- **Never push directly to `main`.** Every change goes through a branch
  and a pull request. Real branch-protection enforcement of this depends
  on your GitHub plan and repo visibility — private repos on the free tier
  do not get GitHub-enforced branch protection (confirmed limit, see this
  kit's `LESSONS-LEARNED.md`). **If that's your situation, don't rely on
  discipline alone — pair this rule with `templates/pre-push-hook.template`**
  (a local git hook that mechanically rejects a direct push to `main`
  from any clone that has it wired, regardless of whether an agent's
  context happens to include this rule at the time). Written policy
  plus memory is exactly what a real incident showed can lapse on some
  changes while working correctly on others in the same session — see
  `LESSONS-LEARNED.md`'s "Multi-repo / multi-codebase products" section
  for the fullest account, since it's especially sharp when the product
  spans more than one repo and this rule lives in a repo the current
  session isn't rooted in.
- **Never force-push** to `main` or to a branch you didn't create.
- **Never delete a branch or PR that isn't yours** without being
  explicitly told to.
- **Any destructive git operation** — `reset --hard`, history rewrite,
  branch deletion, force-push — needs explicit human approval **in that
  moment**. Never automatic, never because it seemed like the convenient
  path forward.

## Secrets and data

- **Never commit `.env`, tokens, credentials, or API keys.** `.gitignore`
  covers the known paths — that's a safety net, not a substitute for
  checking your own diff before pushing.
- **Never commit anything under `{{DATA_DIR}}`** (generated/scraped/large
  data — fill in the real path(s) for this project, or delete this bullet
  if not applicable). Regeneratable data has no reason to live in git
  history, and if it contains any real personal/business data, it has
  legal/privacy weight a git commit shouldn't casually carry.

## Code quality and honesty

- **Every PR touching real logic includes or updates tests**, and the full
  suite passes locally before the PR is opened — not "should pass,"
  actually run and confirmed. Paste the real output in the PR template's
  "Tests run" section.
- **Never fabricate test results, data, or verification claims.** If
  something wasn't checked, say so. The difference between "verified" and
  "assumed" has to survive in code changes, not just in conversation.
- **Every PR that changes behavior, data handling, or schema updates the
  relevant docs in the same PR** — not a follow-up, not "docs later."

## Scope

- **Stay inside the task or issue you were given.** Don't restructure
  unrelated parts of the repo, rename things, or "clean up while you're in
  there" unless that's what was actually asked for. Small, reviewable PRs
  over large speculative ones.

## Finding a bug in the governance kit itself (not in {{PROJECT_NAME}})

This project's `.github/AGENT_RULES.md`, `scripts/setup-hooks.sh`,
`scripts/verify-setup.sh`, and everything else copied in from
`taseenali/Kit-GO` (or whichever copy this project
uses) are, from this repo's point of view, *upstream* — the same
"stay inside the task you were given" scope rule above applies to them,
not just to third-party dependencies in the usual sense.

**Scope of this rule:** it applies to a downstream project using the
kit — like this one. **It does not apply to a session working directly
inside the kit's own repository** (a deliberate kit-maintenance session);
that session's whole job is fixing the kit, so nothing here should be
read as telling it to defer its own work to itself.

**Finding a real bug, gap, or bit of friction in the kit itself while
doing unrelated work on {{PROJECT_NAME}} is not, by itself, a reason to
go open a PR against the kit's own repository.** (The same goes for a
genuinely reusable *idea* — see "This covers more than defects" below.)
Record it in
`docs/KIT-ISSUES-LOG.md` (from `templates/KIT-ISSUES-LOG.md.template`)
instead, and keep working on whatever this project's actual task was.
This is a deliberate change from this kit's own earlier default of
propagating every fix upstream immediately — that default produced real,
useful fixes, but also meant unrelated project work kept turning into
kit maintenance as a side effect, every time, with no separate decision
ever made about whether that was the right use of the moment.

**This covers more than defects.** A genuinely reusable idea this
project builds or discovers — a real improvement or lesson the kit
doesn't have yet, not just something broken — gets the same treatment:
recorded in `docs/KIT-ISSUES-LOG.md`, not turned into an upstream PR as a
side effect of unrelated work. That log's entry format has a note on how
to record an idea that isn't defect-shaped (no symptom, no workaround);
use it rather than forcing an idea into a bug report's shape. The
section's title says "bug" for stability — other files cite it by that
exact heading — but the rule covers both.

- **If `docs/KIT-ISSUES-LOG.md` doesn't exist in this project yet**,
  create it from `templates/KIT-ISSUES-LOG.md.template` first (filling
  in `{{PROJECT_NAME}}`), then record the entry — don't skip recording
  just because the file hasn't been copied in yet, and don't silently
  work around the kit bug with nothing written down anywhere.
- **Tell the human, in the same turn, that you recorded an entry** —
  don't let this happen silently in the background. State plainly: an
  entry was added, and it needs to reach the kit's maintainer somehow
  (a manual share by default, or the optional automated notification if
  this project has it wired — see `docs/KIT-ISSUES-LOG.md`'s own "How an
  entry reaches the kit's maintainer" section for both, and point the
  human at that section rather than re-explaining it inline every time).
  A recorded-but-never-mentioned entry is barely better than one that
  was never recorded at all — the human has to actually know it happened
  to do anything with it.
- **If a collaborator who isn't the kit's maintainer asks for the
  real-time exception below anyway** ("just fix it now, I'll tell them
  later"), decline and record-and-defer instead, the same as if they
  hadn't asked — the exception is the maintainer's own call to make in
  their own session, not something anyone else can authorize on their
  behalf, however reasonable the request sounds in the moment.

**The one exception — fixing it in the kit's repo right now, in this
same session — is only ever the kit maintainer's call, made explicitly,
in the moment.** It is never something an agent decides on its own the
situation "clearly" warrants, even in a session the maintainer is
otherwise directing, and even if the fix is small, obviously correct, or
identical to one already made elsewhere. If that exception is used,
still add the entry to `docs/KIT-ISSUES-LOG.md`, noting that it was
resolved at runtime and by whom — the log should stay a complete record
either way, not just of what got deferred. See
`docs/KIT-ISSUES-LOG.md`'s own header for the mechanical reality behind
this **today**: anyone other than the kit's maintainer almost certainly
doesn't have push access to the kit's repository anyway, so for them
this is as much a hard fact as a policy right now. That's a fact about
the current, single-maintainer state of the kit repo, not a permanent
guarantee — if the kit repo ever gains a second person with write
access, this stops being mechanically true and becomes pure discipline
for them too; don't let the current mechanical backstop read as more
durable than it actually is.

