## Review rigor scales with what's actually at stake

Not every change needs the same scrutiny. A large engineering org
spreading review across multiple people over days or weeks makes sense
there because many people touch the same code without live coordination,
no one person can hold the whole system in their head, and a bad merge has
org-wide blast radius. That often does **not** describe a small or
solo-plus-agent project — copying the *form* of heavy process (artificial
delay, reviewers that don't exist) is theater, not safety. The *principle*
is real and portable: a change should be checked by something other than
whatever wrote it, and how hard it's checked should scale with what
actually breaks if it's wrong. Three tiers:

**No exception for a PR whose framing looks like Tier 0/1.** Any change
that touches this file (this project's `AGENT_RULES.md`),
`.github/CODEOWNERS`, `MODEL-UTILIZATION-PLAYBOOK.md`, this project's
auto-loaded `CLAUDE.md`/`AGENTS.md`, `.github/workflows/**`/
`.githooks/**` (this project's own CI and git-native enforcement
mechanisms — as load-bearing as any of the files above, and this
applies to every author, not just a bot editing them — see "Bot/CI-
authored PRs" below), or, if this project installed Kit-GO,
`docs/KIT-GO.md`, `scripts/kit-go.sh` and `.claude/**` (agent-facing
command definitions that grant pre-approved command execution — see the
Tier 2 definition below) — including a change that is *only* about how the
tiers themselves work, a one-line CODEOWNERS edit that looks like pure
config, or a playbook or CLAUDE.md edit that looks like "just" a
wording tweak — is Tier 2 per the list below, unconditionally, the
moment it touches any of these files. The tier a PR's own commit
message or title implies is not authoritative; what the diff actually
touches is.

**Tier 0 — Low stakes.** Docs-only changes, wording fixes, non-binding
clarifications. **The full test suite is run and reported green before
self-merge — unconditionally, not "if applicable."** A PR *labeled*
docs-only is exactly the framing a change that quietly also touches code
(or touches a doc a test asserts on) would hide behind, and a path-
filtered CI workflow never triggers at all on a pure-docs diff — so
"CI didn't run" cannot stand in for "tests passed" here (see "Self-merge
preconditions" below). Running the suite is cheap; skipping it on a
labeling assumption is the actual risk. **Self-merged by the agent**
once the suite is green, no human click needed.

**Tier 1 — Real changes.** New features, application logic. Full suite
must pass, AND get an independent review pass before merge — a **fresh**
invocation of an AI code-review pass (not the same continuous conversation
that just wrote the change immediately confirming its own work), or a
second person if one is actually available. That distinction — fresh vs.
same-context — is the real mechanism, not a formality: the value of
"someone else checks it" comes from a genuinely separate, decorrelated
pass with no stake in the implementation already being right. A fresh
review pass gets you most of that even from the same underlying model.
**Self-merged by the agent once that fresh pass is clean (zero unresolved
confirmed findings) and CI is green — no human click required.** A human
clicking "approve" on something they haven't actually reviewed isn't a
second check, it's theater that's worse than not having the step at all —
if a project finds itself in that pattern, the fix is to make Tier 1
self-merge explicit (as here), not to keep the empty click. The agent
still reports what merged, in a summary, so the human stays informed
without having to act on every PR.

**Tier 2 — Load-bearing / high blast radius.** Database schema changes,
{{LOAD_BEARING_LOGIC_DESCRIPTION}} (name the specific fragile/critical
logic in *this* project — a pricing engine, an auth check, a data-
correction rule, whatever it actually is here), anything touching real
user/customer data handling, secrets, legal-compliance-adjacent behavior,
or this document itself, `.github/CODEOWNERS` (the file that
defines who Tier 2 sign-off goes to — an agent must not be able to
self-merge a change to its own accountable human; list it here
explicitly, don't rely on a reader inferring it), or
`MODEL-UTILIZATION-PLAYBOOK.md` (governs model selection for every task
in this project, including Tier 2 review itself — a corrupted edit to it
is exactly as load-bearing as one to this document), this project's
auto-loaded `CLAUDE.md`/`AGENTS.md` (whatever an agent reads automatically
at session start governs every task in this project just as much as the
playbook does — the same reasoning, applied to a different file), or
`.github/workflows/**`/`.githooks/**` (this project's own git-native and
CI enforcement mechanisms — if this project has no platform-level branch
protection, these files ARE the primary enforcement, not a backup, so a
corrupted or weakened edit to either is exactly as load-bearing as one
to this document; applies to every author, human or bot — see "Bot/CI-
authored PRs" below for why a bot's own edits here don't get an easier
tier), or, **if this project installed Kit-GO**, `docs/KIT-GO.md` (the
playbook agents follow when a user types `Kit-GO`), `scripts/kit-go.sh`
(auto-run by the slash commands), and `.claude/**` (slash-command files
carry `allowed-tools` pre-approvals — a change to them can make an agent
execute something without a prompt): the same reasoning as `MODEL-UTILIZATION-PLAYBOOK.md`
and `CLAUDE.md`/`AGENTS.md` above — whatever an agent reads or runs
automatically governs every task, so an edit to it cannot be an agent-
self-merged "wording tweak". Tier 1's fresh review
pass, PLUS: if a multi-agent/deeper-review mode is available and
user-triggerable (several independent reasoning processes on the same
diff — the closest available analog to multiple developers reviewing),
**it's required by default, not merely recommended** — the agent requests
it and waits for the result rather than recommending it once and moving
on. The agent still cannot self-invoke it if doing so requires the user's
own action/billing. **Three real outcomes here, not two:** it runs and
its findings get addressed; it's genuinely unavailable **and the human
has confirmed that, not just the agent** (see "Local multi-pass review"
below for exactly what confirmation means) and the fallback stands in
instead, clearly labeled as such; or the human explicitly waives **real
deeper review of any kind — the tool AND the fallback, not one or the
other** — for a specific PR they judge doesn't need either. A waiver of
"the tool only" that leaves the fallback owed is not a real category —
if the human wants the free local option instead of paying, that's
outcome two, not a waiver. Any waiver is **narrow by design**: stated at
the time, for that one PR only, never a standing policy, never inferred
from a prior PR's run. Routine waiving is a signal to revisit
the Tier 2 boundary itself, not evidence the requirement was too strict.
Tier 2 also requires **explicit, informed human sign-off before merge — never
self-merged by an agent, deeper review or not** (what that means exactly —
the agent never executes the merge itself, even after the human says
yes — is spelled out in "What 'never self-merged by an agent' actually
means" below; read it, the short phrase alone is ambiguous). "Informed" matters: the
agent presents a risk summary in the "Risk summary — required format"
below (what changed, what was checked, residual risk, a recommendation,
and what it would still need to check if challenged) instead of a raw diff, so the human's decision is
real and fast, not a blind click on something nobody read. The part that
actually matters: if something about a Tier 2 change is genuinely unclear
or contested, **it's allowed to sit unresolved** rather than get merged on
a deadline. A slow Tier 2 change is a smaller mistake than a rushed one.

### What "never self-merged by an agent" actually means (Tier 2)

**It means the agent never executes the merge itself — not "the agent
may merge once the human says yes."** The short phrase is genuinely
ambiguous between those two readings (is it the *decision* or the
*execution* that must not be the agent's?), and it has caused the same
real incident independently on more than one project: an agent that could
state the rule correctly on demand still treated a human's informal "yes,
merge it" / "go ahead" as authorization to run the merge command itself.
Informal sign-off language approves the **decision** to merge. It does not
authorize the **agent** to be the one who executes it — those are
different things, and the first does not imply the second.

Concretely, for a Tier 2 PR:

- The agent's last action is the risk summary (see "Risk summary —
  required format" below) and, once the human signs off, the exact
  ready-to-run command (e.g. `gh pr merge <number> --squash
  --delete-branch --match-head-commit <the-reviewed-commit-sha>`) for the
  human to run in their own terminal. The `--match-head-commit` pin makes
  GitHub refuse the merge if the branch has moved since the commit that
  was reviewed. **The agent pushes nothing to the branch after sign-off;**
  any new commit — including a branch update or rebase onto the base —
  voids the sign-off and restarts the protocol from the review step.
- **The agent does not merge the PR itself, and does not cause it to end
  up merged, by any mechanism — not after a verbal "yes," not after
  "please merge it," not even after an explicit, direct instruction to
  do so.** "By any mechanism" is load-bearing, not filler: it covers
  `gh pr merge`, enabling GitHub's auto-merge (the agent triggers it,
  GitHub merges later — not a technicality that satisfies this rule), a
  direct REST/GraphQL call to the merge endpoint, driving the merge
  button through a browser tool, delegating the merge to another tool, a
  subagent, or a CI workflow the agent authored, and landing the same
  change on the default branch some other way (a direct push, a
  fast-forward) that isn't literally "merging the PR" but has the
  identical effect. If the change ends up on the default branch and the
  agent set that in motion, it's covered, whichever tool or intermediary
  ran the last step.
- This is a hard rule with no situational exception an agent gets to
  recognize on its own. If a human wants it changed, that's an edit to
  this document itself (Tier 2, same protocol, and the same restriction
  applies to merging *that* PR).
- **Honest limit: this is agent discipline, not a mechanical control.**
  A post-hoc check on who merged (the PR API's `merged_by`) cannot tell a
  human click from an agent running `gh pr merge` when the agent's CLI
  session authenticates as the same GitHub account as the human — the
  common solo-owner setup (see `PASS-0-ENVIRONMENT-DISCOVERY.md` §4). A
  real technical backstop needs a separate identity for agent-initiated
  git operations, which is its own design decision, not something this
  paragraph provides. Some agent hosts also block an unreviewed
  agent-initiated merge at the tool-call level; that is a property of
  that host, not of this document, and shouldn't be assumed present
  elsewhere.

### Risk summary — required format

Every Tier 2 risk summary (and any other "ready for sign-off"
presentation) has all five parts below, in this order. Part 5 is not
optional and not satisfied by a checklist-complete summary alone. For
Tier 0/1 self-merges there is no sign-off presentation, so **part 5
alone is required** — in the PR body and again in the after-merge
report (see "Self-merge preconditions" below).

1. **What changed.**
2. **What was checked** — and how (a real command and result, not "looks
   fine").
3. **Residual risk** — what could still go wrong.
4. **Recommendation.**
5. **If challenged — what I'd still need to check.** Before presenting
   anything as ready, ask: if the human pushed back right now with "are
   you sure it's ready and safe?", what would I actually go and find?
   Write down the real answer — specific unverified claims, paths never
   executed, interactions with other open work not traced, anything that
   was only read or reasoned about rather than run. If the honest answer
   is "nothing," say what was ruled out and how, rather than leaving the
   section blank or writing "none."

Part 5 runs, not just exists (and for Tier 2, the whole summary is
re-presented, not just part 5): (a) the first time anything is
presented as ready; (b) again after **every** fix commit made in response
to a review finding, before re-presenting (a fix is exactly when
confidence quietly resets to "should be fine now" without being
re-earned); and (c) immediately before a Tier 0/1 self-merge, since time
and further commits can pass between "ready" and "merging." **Honest
limit:** whether the agent really interrogated itself is not mechanically
checkable from outside — what *is* checkable is that this section is
present, specific, and not boilerplate. A reviewer or human reading a
generic or empty section should treat that as a defect in the summary.

### Self-merge preconditions and transparency (Tier 0/1)

Before an agent self-merges anything, ALL of the following hold:

- **The full suite has actually been run and reported green for this
  exact change** — via CI when the PR's changed paths trigger it, or run
  locally with the real output pasted into the PR otherwise. "CI didn't
  trigger for these paths" is never read as "tests passed." This
  includes Tier 0: a docs-only PR is the case where path-filtered CI most
  often never runs at all.
- The "If challenged — what I'd still need to check" question (see "Risk
  summary — required format" above) has actually been asked and
  answered, immediately before merging, not just at first presentation.
- For Tier 1: a fresh review pass has actually run against this PR's
  **final head commit** — including any fix commit made in response to an
  earlier pass's findings; fixing findings does not inherit the review
  that found them — and reports zero unresolved confirmed findings (a plausible-but-uncertain finding
  gets a documented judgment call, not silent dismissal).
- The tier assignment is still correct as of the final diff — see "Tier
  re-evaluation" below.

**After merging** (not a precondition — this stays separate from the
"before" list above on purpose): the agent reports what happened in the
same turn — what merged, why, what the fresh review checked, and the
answer to the "if challenged" question above — so a self-merge is always
visible, never silent.

### Tier re-evaluation

A PR's tier is decided by its **final diff content**, not by what it was
expected to touch when the branch was opened. When a change is genuinely
ambiguous between two tiers, **default to the higher one** — treating a
Tier 1 change as Tier 2 costs one extra review pass; treating a Tier 2
change as Tier 1 skips the one thing (human accountability) that can't be
recovered after the fact.

### Bot/CI-authored PRs

A PR opened by a bot account (a dependency-update bot, a deploy bot, any
non-human, non-primary-agent actor) is evaluated by **the exact same
Tier rules as any other author, based on what the diff actually
touches** — there is no separate, easier tier for a bot. A dependency
version bump is real, executable code (arbitrary code can run at
install time via package lifecycle scripts), not a docs-only change —
it does not qualify for Tier 0 just because a bot wrote it. An earlier
draft of this rule proposed exactly that carve-out (Tier 0 for "a
dependency bump with passing CI") and a fresh + adversarial review pair
caught two real problems with it before it shipped: it contradicts this
document's own core principle that a change should be "checked by
something other than whatever wrote it" (a bot's own passing CI is not
that check), and "passing CI" is often a hollow qualifier in a project
that has no real test-running CI job yet — don't let a green check that
only verifies "a PR exists" or "a workflow ran" be mistaken for "this
change was actually reviewed." A compromised or malicious dependency
update auto-merged with zero review is a real, not hypothetical, supply
chain risk. Treat a bot's PR with the same scrutiny as a human's,
scaled to what it actually changes — often Tier 1 (needs a fresh review
pass) at minimum for anything beyond pure lockfile/version-string
changes, and Tier 2 if it touches any of the files already named as
unconditionally Tier 2 in the "Tier 2 — Load-bearing / high blast
radius" definition above — that list applies to every author, bot
included; it isn't restated here to avoid the two copies drifting apart
(a bot bumping a pinned GitHub Action version inside a workflow file is
still touching this project's own governance infrastructure, not a
docs-only change, however routine it looks).

### Tier 2 approval protocol

1. Fresh review pass, findings fixed or explicitly triaged.
2. Deeper/multi-agent review requested and completed, **or, if it's
   genuinely unavailable and the human has confirmed that** (billing, a
   broken connector — see "Local multi-pass review" below for what
   confirmation actually requires) and doesn't want to pay to unblock it,
   the **local multi-pass fallback** below — or the human explicitly
   waives real deeper review of any kind (the tool AND the fallback) for
   that specific PR, stated at the time. Three real options, not two;
   unavailability is not automatically a waiver, and an agent's own
   belief that the tool is unavailable is not the same as the human
   confirming it.
3. Any findings (from step 2 or the fallback) verified against the real code, not trusted
   blind, and fixed or explicitly declined with reasoning. **A fix
   written in response to findings is itself a new change: the fresh
   review in step 1 applies again, separately, to each fix commit — not
   just to the original change.** Fixing findings does not inherit the
   review that found them; whether the fix actually did what the findings
   called for, without introducing a new problem, has never been checked
   until something other than the agent that wrote it checks it. This is
   most important for self-referential changes (a document that governs
   the review process itself), where a subtle, well-intentioned fix can
   quietly open exactly the gap the original review was closing.
4. Agent writes a risk summary in the "Risk summary — required format"
   above: what changed, what was verified, what the residual risk is, a
   direct recommendation, **and what it would still need to check if
   challenged.**
5. Human reads the summary and makes the actual call — never skipped,
   rushed, or delegated to another agent (see the table below:
   accountability that requires a named human is the one thing this
   whole tier system cannot manufacture out of more review). **The human
   then runs the merge themselves** — see "What 'never self-merged by an
   agent' actually means" above; the agent's work ends at the risk
   summary and the ready-to-run command.

### Local multi-pass review — the deeper-review fallback

**This is the Tier 2 review instance of a general rule — see
`MODEL-UTILIZATION-PLAYBOOK.md` for the full framework covering model
choice across every task in this project, not just this one case.** The
two documents govern the same territory from different angles (review
rigor vs. model choice) and must never disagree — a conflict between
them is a bug to fix on sight.

A deeper/multi-agent review mode is often a real, billed service — it can
become genuinely unavailable (usage credits exhausted, a connector
broken) with no fix the agent can perform. When that happens and the
human doesn't want to spend to unblock it, this is the fallback for this
section's own step 2 — real and defined, not an ad hoc "just do another
fresh pass":

1. **Confirm unavailability with the human before doing anything else —
   a one-shot failure is not "genuinely unavailable."** A single failed
   invocation (a timeout, a transient error) is not sufficient on its own
   to declare it unavailable and move to the fallback — report the real
   failure and ask the human whether to retry, unblock it, or use the
   fallback. The one exception: the human has already stated directly
   that it's unavailable/they won't pay for it — that statement itself is
   the confirmation, nothing further to ask. What's never acceptable: an
   agent unilaterally deciding "unavailable" from its own side and
   proceeding straight to the fallback without the human's actual
   knowledge or say.
2. The existing fresh review pass (already required, this checklist's
   own step 1 above) stands as-is.
3. **Before running anything on a different model, tell the human
   plainly**: that a model switch is about to happen, and a one-line
   reason why. This step comes *before* the pass it describes, not
   after — never a silent model change, then a mention.
4. **One additional pass on a model that's actually different from
   whatever ran step 2**, if the agent framework supports a model
   override for a sub-invocation — explicitly instructed to review
   adversarially: hunt for problems, don't confirm the work. The
   requirement is *difference from step 2's model*, not a fixed choice —
   if the calling session is already running on the model you'd default
   to for this pass, use a different one instead, or the two passes share
   the same blind spots and the fallback's whole point is lost. This is
   the substitution that actually matters: the same model re-prompted
   still shares that model's blind spots; a genuinely different model
   checking the same diff is real decorrelation, closer to what multiple
   independent developers (or a real multi-agent review mode) provides
   than any amount of same-model re-prompting.
5. Findings from both passes deduped and combined, verified against the
   real code exactly like this checklist's own step 3 would treat them.
6. **The risk summary explicitly labels this "local multi-pass fallback,
   not [the deeper review tool]"** — never presented as equivalent. Name
   the actual models that ran, never a fixed phrase copied regardless of
   what actually ran — stating a model that didn't run is a fabricated
   verification claim.

What this still doesn't reach — true of this two-model fallback exactly
as much as any single-agent pass, since neither is a human: independent
human knowledge, bus factor, and accountability that requires a named
human. It closes more of the "multiple independent reasoning processes"
gap than same-model multi-pass would, but it's not the real deeper-review
tool, and the risk summary must say so.

### Maturity triggers — when this whole policy must be revisited

The tier boundaries and self-merge authority above are calibrated to
*this project's current state* — fill in what that actually is (solo
owner? small team? nothing in production yet?). That calibration is not
permanent; it's the current entry in a living progress graph. Trigger
conditions to revisit it explicitly, rather than assume it's fine
forever — edit this list to fit the real project:

- Real production deployment / live customer-facing traffic.
- A second human, or an independently-operating agent session, joins —
  Tier 1's self-merge authority in particular is calibrated to a solo,
  synchronous, full-context situation that stops holding once it isn't.
  **Don't detect this by inferring who is at the keyboard from session
  or environment fields.** A field like the session context's
  `userEmail` (or a git `user.email`, an OS username, an account that
  changed partway through a session) reflects the local account/session
  context, not necessarily a different real person. A real session took a
  changed `userEmail` at face value, told the owner a second contributor
  had joined, and wrongly invoked this very trigger — it was the same
  person on a different local account. The real signals are a PR or review created by that person's own GitHub
  account (the authenticated actor — not commit author metadata, which
  comes from the same local `user.email`), and only if no agent shares
  that login (see `PASS-0-ENVIRONMENT-DISCOVERY.md` §4), or an explicit
  statement from the human; when it matters and neither is
  available, ask rather than guess.
  **This is also the trigger for building real mechanical detection of
  who actually merged a Tier 2 PR** (a `merged_by`-vs-`CODEOWNERS`
  check, see `LESSONS-LEARNED.md`'s "second-human self-merge" entry) —
  don't build that mechanism before this trigger fires. On a solo-owner
  project it would be structurally unable to produce a single true
  positive (author, merger, and sole owner are always the same login),
  which is worse than not having it: a check that can never turn red
  reads as coverage that doesn't exist (found by a fresh + adversarial
  review pair, 2026-09-23, reviewing a plan that tried to build this
  mechanism too early).
- Data scope expands to include anything with its own privacy/compliance
  weight this project didn't have before.
- The first hired contributor whose access needs real boundaries.
- The first real transaction/dollar moves through the system.

### What "review by someone other than the author" actually buys you — and what a single agent can and can't reach

Worth naming honestly rather than assuming AI review is either a full
substitute or worthless. In practice, multiple human developers on a team
now very often each work with their own AI agent too — so "a different
developer reviewed it" is frequently, in practice, already "a different AI
session, with different context, reviewed it." What actually matters was
never strictly human-vs-AI; it's whether the check ran as a genuinely
separate process with an incentive to find problems, not the same context
that produced the change nodding at itself.

| What real independent review provides | Reachable with a fresh single-agent pass?¹ |
|---|---|
| Breaks anchoring bias (no stake in the implementation being right) | **Yes** — a fresh invocation, not the authoring context |
| Adversarial incentive (looks for problems, not confirmation) | **Yes** — if explicitly instructed to hunt for problems |
| Time/context decorrelation (not mid-flow on the same problem) | **Yes** — a separate invocation |
| Multiple independent reasoning processes on the same diff | **Partially, and not by a single pass**¹ — a multi-agent review mode gets closer, but if it needs the user's own trigger/billing, don't assume it's always run. When it's genuinely unavailable (and the human has confirmed that), the local multi-pass fallback — two coordinated passes on two different models, not one — closes more of this than same-model re-prompting would, though still less than a real multi-agent mode |
| A second human's independent lived knowledge / org memory | **No** — not achievable by AI review at all, no matter how many passes or which model(s) run them; the gap is "not a human," not "same model twice" |
| Bus factor / knowledge diffusion across actual people | **No** — only a real second person reading the change does this |
| Accountability that specifically requires a named human | **No** — this is exactly why Tier 2 keeps explicit human sign-off regardless of how good the AI review is |

¹ Every other row in this table is genuinely reachable by one fresh pass.
The "Multiple independent reasoning processes" row is the deliberate
exception — its own answer says "not by a single pass," and the local
multi-pass fallback that partially reaches it is explicitly two
coordinated passes on two different models. Don't read this column
header as claiming that row's partial answer comes from one pass too.

**So:** a fresh, adversarially-framed review pass is a real, achievable
substitute for Tier 1 — not a stand-in to skip, an actual mechanism that
closes most of the anchoring-bias gap, and specifically what makes Tier
1's self-merge authority (above) a real safeguard rather than nothing at
all. It is **not** a substitute for Tier 2's human sign-off, because the
remaining gaps (independent human knowledge, accountability) aren't
things more or better AI review can close — no agent, fresh context or
not, changes that. If real collaborators join later, Tier 1's self-merge
authority is one of the specific things to revisit — see "Maturity
triggers" above, and any other provisional limit noted in this document
(branch protection, etc.) gets the same honest re-check, not left
silently assumed fine forever.

## Definition of Done — every standing practice, every time

Every project accumulates practices that never got written down as rules —
a docs-sync habit, a place answers get archived, a testing ritual — and
because they're unwritten, they're the first things to slip under time
pressure, even after being followed correctly many times in a row. This
section exists to close that gap: once a practice is real and repeated on
this project, it graduates onto this checklist and gets the same binding
status as the git/secrets/testing rules above — checked every time a task
or PR is reported done, not weighed against convenience, not skipped
because it wasn't the stated focus of the current task.

**Starter checklist — edit to match what this project actually does:**

- [ ] **{{ANSWER_ARCHIVE_LOCATION}}** — if the user asked a question or got
  a substantive explanation/guide/decision this turn, it's saved to the
  project's answer archive (if one exists) in the same turn, not deferred.
- [ ] **Docs sync.** If this change affects what the system does, what it
  knows, or how it's used, the relevant docs are updated in the same PR —
  never "docs later."
- [ ] **Collaborator log updated** (if this project uses one — see
  `docs/collaborators/<your-username>.md`, `templates/COLLABORATOR-LOG.md.template`).
  A dated entry, signed, naming whether an AI agent did the work and
  which one — not a restatement of the PR title.
- [ ] **Open research queue checked** (see `OPEN-RESEARCH-QUEUE.md`, if
  this project uses one). Before starting any pass sized beyond a small
  fix, check it for anything worth a fresh attempt — research stays ahead
  of the build, not reactive to it. A stalled question is never described
  as "exhausted" permanently, only "no new angle found yet." Update the
  queue with any new attempt's real result, resolved or not.
- [ ] **A mixed batch of findings/requests is triaged explicitly, not
  answered as one undifferentiated pile.** When a turn surfaces several
  things at once, the response itself names, for each one, which of
  three categories it's in — already confirmed fine and needing nothing,
  genuinely actionable, or real but deliberately deferred — clearly
  enough that a reader could check the categorization without
  re-deriving it themselves. That naming is the actual, checkable gate
  this item requires: read the turn back — does it say, explicitly,
  which category each item landed in, or does the reader have to infer
  it from what got acted on? How the response is then organized around
  that naming (direct answers before a plan, actionable items grouped
  into phases when the work has a real sequence) is good practice worth
  doing, but is guidance, not a second gate this checklist item enforces
  — the requirement is the honest categorization, not a specific layout
  for presenting it. Only the categorization half is kept to the "small,
  safe default" standard this kit applies before adding a standing
  rule (see this kit's own `LESSONS-LEARNED.md` — a downstream project
  copy of this document won't have that file locally, but the test
  itself is short enough to restate here: a standing "always do X" rule
  is only safe to leave standing if X is genuinely small and low-cost;
  naming a category is, laying out a whole response structure a
  particular way is a bigger claim than this item makes).
- [ ] **Tests actually run.** The real suite was executed this turn, and
  the count/result quoted is what actually came back — not estimated, not
  carried over from memory of an earlier run.
- [ ] **Review tier assigned and honored** per "Review rigor" above,
  including Tier 1's fresh review pass actually invoked and Tier 2's full
  approval protocol (deeper review — or, if genuinely unavailable **and
  the human has confirmed that, not just the agent's own belief**, the
  local multi-pass fallback, clearly labeled as such — risk summary
  including its "If challenged" part, real human sign-off, and the
  merge command handed to the human rather than run by the agent — see
  "What 'never self-merged by an agent' actually means") actually
  followed — not just recommended and left there.
- [ ] **Branch → PR → CI → merge**, no direct push to `main` — including
  for changes that feel too trivial to bother. Tier 0/1 self-merge once
  their preconditions hold, reported to the human afterward, not asked as
  a question first. Bigger PRs covering more real work per pass are fine
  — but each merges promptly on its own once ready; never hold multiple
  PRs open to merge collectively, which only grows conflict risk the
  longer it's deferred.
- [ ] **A kit bug — or a genuinely reusable idea the kit doesn't have
  yet — is recorded, not fixed in passing.** If this task surfaced a real
  bug, gap, or reusable improvement/lesson in the governance kit itself
  (not in this project's own domain), it's recorded in
  `docs/KIT-ISSUES-LOG.md` before the task is called done — see "Finding a
  bug in the governance kit itself" above (the same file and rule cover a
  good idea worth the kit's attention, not just a defect). It is *not*, by default, propagated into a PR against
  the kit's own repository as a side effect of this task; that only
  happens when the kit's maintainer explicitly directs it in the moment,
  and even then the log still gets the entry, marked as resolved at
  runtime.
- [ ] **Durable-guidance capture.** If the user gave guidance this turn
  that should hold beyond this single conversation (a correction, a
  confirmed approach, a standing fact), it's captured somewhere that
  survives the conversation ending — a memory system, a rules file,
  wherever this project's agents actually persist that kind of thing — not
  left for a future session to rediscover the same ground.
- [ ] **Honesty check.** Every claim of "done," "confirmed," "N tests
  passing," or "verified" in this turn's summary is something actually
  checked in this turn — not inferred, not remembered, not assumed still
  true from earlier in the conversation.

**Why this exists, plainly:** the failure mode isn't any single missed
item — it's a real one that's individually small and gets caught, over and
over, precisely because it never got written down as something to check.
Catching misses after the fact doesn't scale. Once you notice yourself
following an unwritten practice for the second or third time on a project,
that's the signal to add it to this list rather than trust yourself (or the
next agent) to remember it forever.

## Escalation

If a rule above conflicts with what you're being asked to do, or you're
genuinely unsure whether something crosses a line here, stop and ask a
human owner (`.github/CODEOWNERS`) rather than deciding on your own that
this case is an exception.
